; A DEL byte (0x7F) in a payee or account counts as zero columns when
; Ledger pads report fields, like any other control character (#3291).
;
; The payee below is "Pay", DEL, "ee" and the account "Expenses:Fo", DEL,
; "od"; the DEL bytes are invisible in most editors.  Ledger measures a
; field by the display width of its characters; a control character
; draws nothing, so the 6-byte payee measures 5 columns and a 20-column
; field adds 15 spaces (the 14-byte account: 13 columns, 7 spaces), which
; is how a terminal shows it.  Before #3291 DEL measured -1 column, one
; space more per field.  Text that is all printable ASCII is measured by
; its length alone, and DEL must not count as printable there: counted
; as one ordinary column it would cost each field a space and move reg's
; later columns left.

2024/01/01 Payee
    Expenses:Food    $10.00
    Assets:Cash

test reg
24-Jan-01 Payee                 Expenses:Food                $10.00       $10.00
                                Assets:Cash                 $-10.00            0
end test

test reg --format '|%-20(payee)|%-20(account)|\n'
|Payee               |Expenses:Food       |
|Payee               |Assets:Cash         |
end test
